; metacalc Z80 canvas for the TI-84 Plus SE. Draws into a 96 by 64 frame buffer in RAM that
; matches crates/mcui/src/canvas.rs, and copies it to the LCD. Nothing else draws.
;
; Code runs from flash and is position dependent. Assembling this file alone places it at
; $4000, with -I at ui, at the repository root and at app/src so that "ui/ram.inc" and
; ram_map.inc are found; an including file defines UI_EMBEDDED first and places the code itself.
; The glyph table is generated: `mcglyph z80 font.inc`, found through the include path as
; "font.inc".
;
; RAM, two blocks placed by app/src/ram_map.inc, where UI_FRAME and UI_VARS can be moved: the
; frame, 768 bytes at UI_FRAME, default $9340 (plotSScreen), row-major, 12 bytes a row, the most
; significant bit leftmost; and UI_VARS_SIZE ($1A, 26) bytes of variables at UI_VARS, default
; $86EC (saveSScreen), laid out in ram.inc. The blocks must not overlap another region of the
; map. Only ui_fontp holds anything between calls, and the OS zeroes saveSScreen when auto power
; down fires in _GetKey, so call ui_set_font again after any key wait that can power the
; calculator down. Call ui_set_font and ui_clear before the first draw. Registers not listed as
; destroyed are preserved, and IX, IY and the shadow registers are never touched. Counting
; the caller's return address, the stack needs 14 bytes for ui_list_row, 12 for ui_title and
; ui_soft_keys, 10 for the text-right and centred routines, 8 for ui_bar, 32 for ui_truncate,
; 22 for ui_tail and 44 for ui_wrap; an interrupt adds the handler's pushes.
;
; Fonts: every routine that draws or measures text reads the table given to ui_set_font. A
; table is what mcglyph z80 writes: a slot of 8 bytes per glyph, the width then seven row
; bytes, with bit 7 of the width set when row 5 or 6 has a pixel; a glyph nine wide takes a
; second slot of its ninth pixels. The space slot, code 1, is never flagged. Strings are glyph codes
; ended by 0 and code 1 is the space. The soft key row sets the ui_key_font table itself and restores the font in use afterwards; its
; label strings use that table's codes, which differ from the main font's.
;
; Text rows are 0 to 7 and eight scanlines each; a glyph's seven rows start one scanline
; below the top. Pixels outside the frame are dropped as the model drops them. Cutting a
; string to a width is ui_truncate, ui_tail and ui_wrap; folding is decided by the caller.
;
; GLYPH_ADV, a macro in glyph.inc, is the advance of one glyph code in A; ui_text_width and the
; fitting routines expand it.
;
; Routines (details at each): ui_set_font, ui_clear, ui_text, ui_text_width, ui_text_right,
; ui_text_centered, ui_truncate, ui_tail, ui_wrap, ui_invert, ui_invert_row, ui_marker,
; ui_title, ui_list_row, ui_soft_keys, ui_bar, ui_blit. ui_blit waits on the busy flag, bit 7 of
; port 10h, before every LCD write, runs with interrupts off and restores the caller's state,
; and leaves the controller in 8 bit mode with increment mode 5, as TI-OS expects it.

#ifndef UI_EMBEDDED
.org $4000
#endif

#include "ui/ram.inc"
#include "glyph.inc"
#include "shapes.inc"
#include "bars.inc"
#include "progress.inc"
#include "blit.inc"
#include "font.inc"
#include "fit.inc"
